\begin{proof}
If $x^2+1$ were reducible over $\mathbb{F}_7$, then, since it has degree $2$, it would have a linear factor and hence a root in $\mathbb{F}_7$. The squares in $\mathbb{F}_7$ are $0,1,2,4$, so $x^2$ can never equal $-1=6$ in $\mathbb{F}_7$. Thus $x^2+1$ has no root in $\mathbb{F}_7$ and is irreducible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
A quadratic polynomial over a field is reducible if and only if it has a root in that field. For $a\in\mathbb{F}_7$, the possible values of $a^2$ are $0,1,2,4$. Since $-1\equiv 6\pmod 7$ is not among these values, $a^2+1\ne 0$ for every $a\in\mathbb{F}_7$. Therefore $x^2+1$ has no root in $\mathbb{F}_7$, and hence it is irreducible in $\mathbb{F}_7[x]$.
\end{proof}
